Climate change poses a significant threat to the environment and human society, and it has been recognized as a global challenge. The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) reports that global warming is the result of human activities, such as burning fossil fuels, deforestation, and agriculture. To mitigate the effects of climate change, governments worldwide have implemented policies aimed at reducing greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions, phasing out fossil fuels, and transitioning to renewable energy sources. This essay critically evaluates the effectiveness of current climate change policies implemented by governments worldwide and their impact on mitigating the adverse effects of global warming.\n\nScope of Current Climate Change Policies\n\nSeveral countries worldwide have implemented climate change policies with various scopes. The policies range from setting targets for emissions reduction to promoting renewable energy development, among others. For instance, the European Union (EU) has set a target of net-zero emissions by 2050, and it has adopted several measures such as the Emissions Trading System, which caps CO2 emissions from power plants and industries. Similarly, China has set a target of net-zero emissions by 2060, and it has implemented policies such as the Renewable Energy Law, which promotes the development of renewable energy sources such as wind, solar, and hydro. In contrast, the United States, under the Trump administration, withdrew from the Paris Agreement and abolished several climate change policies.\n\nAlignment with International Climate Goals\n\nThe Paris Agreement, signed in 2015, aims to keep the temperature increase below 2°C above pre-industrial levels and pursue efforts to limit the temperature increase to 1.5°C. Current climate change policies implemented by governments worldwide should align with the Paris Agreement's goals to be effective in mitigating the adverse effects of global warming. However, several countries have n